description:
Parse and print keyboard shortcuts for a run-time accelerator table, find conflicts, and read menu mnemonics:

```cpp
struct Shortcut { int key{ 0 }; bool ctrl{ false }, shift{ false }, alt{ false }; };

bool parseShortcut(const std::string& text, Shortcut& out);
std::string formatShortcut(const Shortcut& s);
std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::string>> conflicts(const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::string>>& commands);
char mnemonic(const std::string& menuText);
```

**parseShortcut**: parts separated by `+`, case-insensitive, any order of modifiers; the **last** part is the key. Modifiers are `Ctrl` (or `Control`), `Shift` and `Alt`, each at most once. Keys are a letter or digit (stored as its upper-case character code), `F1`-`F12` (`0x70` onwards), and `Delete` (0x2E), `Insert` (0x2D), `Home` (0x24), `End` (0x23), `PageUp` (0x21), `PageDown` (0x22), `Left` (0x25), `Up` (0x26), `Right` (0x27), `Down` (0x28), `Enter` (0x0D), `Esc` (0x1B), `Space` (0x20), `Tab` (0x09). Spaces around parts are allowed. Anything else fails.

**formatShortcut** writes `Ctrl+Shift+Alt+Key` in that order with the names above (`F5`, `PageUp`, `S`).

**conflicts** takes (command, shortcut text) pairs and returns each pair of command names whose shortcuts are the same, in the order of the first command, then the second. Shortcuts that do not parse are ignored.

**mnemonic**: the upper-case letter after the first single `&` in a menu item's text (`&&` is a literal ampersand), or `0` if there is none.

